=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: The Protein Data Bank


@left


- What information does a Protein Data Bank entry preserve?
- How do structure coordinates, experimental method, and resolution help researchers interpret a model?
- What can a deposited structure show, and what must still be checked?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to structure records](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: The Protein Data Bank


@left


- The PDB is an archive of macromolecular structures with coordinates and associated experimental and biological metadata.
- Entries include structures determined by methods such as X-ray crystallography, NMR, and cryo-electron microscopy.
- Researchers use structures to examine folds, binding sites, complexes, and possible mechanisms.


@top-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to structure records](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: The Protein Data Bank - Reading an entry


@left


- Check the experimental method, resolution or confidence information, biological assembly, and validation notes.
- A coordinate model is an interpretation of evidence; unresolved or flexible regions may be absent or uncertain.
- Structure supports a hypothesis about function but does not by itself prove activity in a living system.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to structure records](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: The Protein Data Bank - What a PDB entry contains


@left


- A structure entry can include atomic coordinates, sequence, experimental method, sample details, and validation information.
- The archive includes proteins, nucleic acids, ligands, and large molecular assemblies.
- Entry metadata links a model to its experimental evidence and related publications.


@top-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to structure records](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: The Protein Data Bank - Read resolution and validation


@left


- For X-ray and cryo-EM structures, resolution describes the level of detail supported by data and model.
- Inspect local quality, model fit, missing regions, stereochemistry, and ligand placement rather than relying on one number.
- Flexible or disordered regions may be absent or poorly resolved.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to structure records](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: The Protein Data Bank - Use structures with context


@left


- Compare sequences and structures to locate conserved sites, possible interfaces, and functional residues.
- A bound ligand or partner suggests an interaction but does not establish biological importance by itself.
- Check species, construct, conditions, and method when using a structure to guide experiments.


@right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 22. X-ray Diffraction and Protein Crystallography


@top-left


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@right


- Crystals
- Diffraction pattern
- Atomic structure
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: X-ray Crystallography


@left


- Why must many protein structures be crystallized before X-ray diffraction?
- How are diffraction measurements used to build an electron-density map?
- How do crystal quality and resolution affect the final structural model?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: X-ray Crystallography


@left


- A crystal arranges many copies of a molecule in an ordered lattice that scatters X-rays into measurable diffraction spots.
- Diffraction intensities and phase information are used to calculate electron density, which guides a structural model.
- Smaller resolution values indicate finer detail, but model quality also depends on data and refinement.


@top-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: X-ray Crystallography - Limits


@left


- Hanging-drop vapor diffusion is one method for screening conditions that encourage protein crystals to form.
- Flexible or heterogeneous proteins may crystallize poorly; crystal packing can also favor one conformation.
- The method provides powerful structural evidence, but crystal quality and model validation remain essential.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: X-ray Diffraction and Protein Crystallography - Grow a protein crystal


@left


- Purify and concentrate a stable protein, then screen buffer, salt, pH, and precipitant conditions.
- In hanging-drop vapor diffusion, a protein drop equilibrates with a larger reservoir and becomes more concentrated over time.
- Flexible, heterogeneous, or unstable samples can be difficult to crystallize.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: X-ray Diffraction and Protein Crystallography - Turn diffraction into a model


@left


- A crystal diffracts X-rays into spots whose positions and intensities contain structural information.
- Computational methods combine diffraction measurements with phase information to calculate an electron-density map.
- A fitted atomic model is refined against the data and checked for geometry and agreement with density.


@top-right


![Student-created workflow from protein preparation to structure determination.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_structure-determination-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: X-ray Diffraction and Protein Crystallography - Read the limits of crystallography


@left


- Higher-resolution data support finer detail, but resolution alone does not guarantee a correct model.
- Radiation damage, crystal disorder, missing density, and flexible domains can limit interpretation.
- The crystal environment may favor one conformation and does not show every state adopted in solution.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to crystals](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 23. Cryo-Electron Microscopy


@top-left


![Student-created cryo-EM workflow from particle images to a three-dimensional model.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-image-processing.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Student-created schematic of the electron path in a cryo-electron microscope.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-instrument.jpg)


@right


- Frozen particles
- Electron images
- 3D reconstruction
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Cryo-EM


@left


- How does rapid freezing preserve a hydrated protein sample for electron microscopy?
- How are many particle images combined into a three-dimensional reconstruction?
- When might cryo-EM suit a sample better than crystallography, and what limits either method?


@top-right


![Student-created cryo-EM workflow from particle images to a three-dimensional model.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-image-processing.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created schematic of the electron path in a cryo-electron microscope.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-instrument.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Cryo-EM


@left


- Samples are rapidly frozen into vitreous ice so particles can be imaged in a near-native hydrated state.
- A detector records many particle views; software aligns, classifies, and combines them into a three-dimensional map.
- Cryo-EM can study large assemblies without growing a crystal, but sample quality, particle behavior, and image processing matter.


@top-right


![Student-created schematic of the electron path in a cryo-electron microscope.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-instrument.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created cryo-EM workflow from particle images to a three-dimensional model.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-image-processing.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Cryo-EM - Interpreting a map


@left


- Preferred orientation or structural flexibility can leave parts of the map poorly resolved.
- Resolution estimates summarize detail at a stated threshold; they do not guarantee equal quality everywhere.
- Cryo-EM and crystallography are complementary tools, not a universal ranking of better and worse.


@top-right


![Student-created cryo-EM workflow from particle images to a three-dimensional model.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-image-processing.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created schematic of the electron path in a cryo-electron microscope.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-instrument.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Cryo-Electron Microscopy - Prepare a cryo-EM specimen


@left


- Apply a small sample drop to a grid and blot away excess liquid to leave a thin film.
- Plunge-freeze the grid rapidly in liquid ethane so water forms vitreous ice rather than damaging crystals.
- The protein should remain dispersed and structurally intact across the grid.


@right


![Student-created schematic of the electron path in a cryo-electron microscope.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-instrument.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Cryo-Electron Microscopy - Reconstruct a 3D structure


@left


- Record many low-dose particle images from different orientations in vitrified ice.
- Software identifies particles, aligns images, groups similar views, and reconstructs a three-dimensional density map.
- Model fitting and validation compare the proposed atomic structure with the reconstructed map.


@right


![Student-created cryo-EM workflow from particle images to a three-dimensional model.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-image-processing.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Cryo-Electron Microscopy - Compare cryo-EM and X-ray methods


@left


- Cryo-EM avoids crystal growth and can suit large assemblies or membrane proteins that are difficult to crystallize.
- Preferred orientation, flexibility, ice thickness, particle quality, and image processing affect the reconstruction.
- Resolution estimates use map comparisons such as Fourier shell correlation and should be read with model validation.


@top-right


![Student-created schematic of the electron path in a cryo-electron microscope.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-instrument.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created cryo-EM workflow from particle images to a three-dimensional model.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_cryo-em-image-processing.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 24. AlphaFold and Protein Structure Prediction


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_086.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)


@right


- Sequence to structure
- Model confidence
- Experimental checks
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Structure Prediction


@left


- How can a structure-prediction system use sequence and learned patterns to propose a protein model?
- What do confidence measures say about local structure and the placement of domains?
- Which biological questions still require experiments after a structure is predicted?


@top-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Structure Prediction


@left


- AlphaFold predicts a plausible three-dimensional structure from sequence using learned relationships in protein data.
- Confidence measures help flag uncertain regions or domain arrangements; they are not experimental resolution values.
- Predictions can accelerate hypotheses about folds and interactions, but do not directly measure activity or cellular context.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_086.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Structure Prediction - Limits


@left


- Flexible, disordered, modified, or ligand-dependent states may be difficult to represent with one predicted structure.
- A model can be useful even when some regions are uncertain; interpret each region according to its confidence.
- Compare predictions with experiment and biological evidence before using them to design a drug or engineered protein.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_086.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: AlphaFold and Protein Structure Prediction - What structure prediction provides


@left


- Prediction systems use an amino-acid sequence and learned patterns from structural data to propose a model.
- Predicted coordinates and confidence measures help prioritize regions and hypotheses for experimental study.
- Uncertainty should be reported and respected even when a prediction is useful.


@top-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: AlphaFold and Protein Structure Prediction - Read confidence and uncertainty


@left

- pLDDT: local structure confidence
- PAE: uncertainty in relative residue or domain placement
- High local confidence does not prove one fixed conformation
- Low confidence can reflect disorder or missing partners

@top-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_086.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: AlphaFold and Protein Structure Prediction - Combine predictions with experiments


@left


- Use predicted structures to plan mutagenesis, identify candidate interfaces, and interpret biochemical results.
- Compare models with sequence conservation, known domains, and structures of related proteins.
- Binding assays, cross-linking, cryo-EM, or crystallography can test important model claims.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to sequence to structure](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_086.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)
